stan in 2007. he had  been released from an impatient  psychiatric facility less than five  months earlier seeing six nova scotia  doctors in the following months  a crisis worker who interviewed desmond  and his wife together told the inquiry  she now questions her own approach i  wonder if i had made sure  to have time alone  if anything if any either of them would  have disclosed anything differently or  it took veterans affairs canada six  months to appoint a case manager for  desmond a delay witnesses blamed on  budget cuts  lawyers will make final submissions in  april a final report with  recommendations is expected this fall  ross lord global news  ahead the pressure of social media on  men how mental health issues intensify  with every post  [Music]  foo fighters drummer taylor hawkins has  died at the age of 50. no cause of death  has been revealed and the band is asking  for privacy for hawkins family at this  time  the kova 19 pandemic has seen canadians  spending more time on their phones than  ever before that involves scrolling  through social media time and time again  as jamie morocco explains it's a habit  that can lead to some major mental  health challenges  scrolling through social media may be a  good distraction for abdullah barez but  it doesn't make him feel great about  himself with every photo he says comes  pressure to be better eat cleaner and  hit the gym harder it kind of gives you  this unrealistic  i guess ideal of what a man should be  though not officially diagnosed perez  described his feelings of insecurity as  body dysmorphia a mental health  condition resulting in an obsessive  negative perception about one's body  research shows in men body image issues  can be linked to anxiety depression and  even suicidal thoughts or behavior and  experts say social media is making the  problem worse  a man might compare himself or his own  body to what he's seeing on social media  which those images are  incredibly unattainable uh filtered  photoshopped could be edited so the  ideal body type becomes increasingly  unrealistic  and exaggerated at the university of  toronto kyle ganson is studying eating  disorders muscle building behaviors and  body image he says almost all of the  people he has interviewed have  referenced influencers or youtubers as  inspiration  these sort of platforms influence their  body image and the desires to change  their body in specific ways once you  start following one bodybuilder or like  a post about dieting an app's algorithms  can fill up a feed with more of the same  marketing expert jenna drenton says  these companies are aware and are  implementing changes  certain hashtags are banned however  consumers are incredibly savvy and find  ways to create different hashtags the  onus then falls largely onto creators to  put out healthy safe and unedited  content and on consumers to educate  themselves and filter who they follow  perhaps easier said than done when any  type of suggested content can still make  it onto one's home page jamie morrocker  global news toronto  next escaping mariupol cut off from the  world and scrounging for food  how a pair of sisters found their way to  safety  [Music]  even if russia pulls back on its goals  in this war one city it may still try to  take is mariupol holding the port city  would give a land corridor between  crimea and the donbass region but  despite constant shelling for the past  month only about half its residents have  left mike armstrong spoke with two  sisters who have now reached safety  every day it was worse and worse they  are two sisters sharing one story about  leaving home we couldn't stay there  this is what they left behind mariupol  basically being reduced to rubble and  cut off without phones or internet twin  sisters anastasia and hannah rechkina  didn't understand what was going on they  thought that maybe if no one is safe  comes to save us maybe the world just  doesn't know about the situation  that's how we saw it  now on the day of the invasion the  sisters say at first they didn't realize  it was happening their city was fought  over in 2014 when russia-backed rebels  took over parts of the donbass region in  the eight years since it was close  enough to the front lines they could  sometimes hear fighting off in the  distance  the explosions when the invasion started  were closer i said mom putin attacked a  maripo now  and then we realized that  it's a real war it's not what we used to  in mariupol  what came next was weeks of scrounging  and hunger in the first days they  rationed the little they had then they  scavenged searching friends homes and  apartments people who'd left looking for  anything we were checking  every shelf if they have something and i  was happy oh my god i found the candle  oh my god i found the  rice or i found something else  the challenge after that was what to do  with what they found without gas or  electricity everything had to be cooked  over a fire out in the open  exposed when you need to go outside to  cook food then there is shelling and you  need to hide there is a constantly sound  of the airplane but you need to go  outside anyways because you cannot  survive without food  the sisters were able to escape they're  still in ukraine but at least safer  their ride out of mariupol was a  stranger who agreed to take their group  just somewhere  i don't know where we where are we going  i don't know how  how much time it will take but i was  happy that i'm i'm in a car i'm  i'm all we are all always with  our family and  that's all i wanted  now there is one thing the sisters feel  guilty about locking the door to their  apartment there isn't much but there is  some food left food they say their  neighbors likely need  desperately  mike armstrong global news krakow poland  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm dallas flex  tonight we leave you in kelowna bc where  members of the local ukrainian community  are rolling up their sleeves preparing  traditional ukrainian treats they're  raising money for medical supplies and  food to help families affected by the  war thanks for watching take care
 

